  "answer_text": "You are likely referring to **Iuka, Mississippi** (with an uppercase \"**I**\"). In many digital sans-serif fonts, the capital \"I\" looks identical to a lowercase \"l,\" leading many people to search for it as \"Luka\".\n\n**Iuka** is a historic and scenic city located in the extreme northeastern corner of Mississippi. It serves as the county seat of Tishomingo County.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Geography & Natural Beauty\n* **Mississippi's Highest Point:** Just south of the city lies **Woodall Mountain**, which is the highest natural point in the state of Mississippi, standing at 806 feet (246 meters) above sea level.\n* **The Landscape:** Unlike much of flat, low-lying Mississippi, Iuka is situated among the rolling hills of the Tennessee River Valley. It is heavily forested and offers a much cooler, more elevated climate compared to the rest of the state. \n* **State Parks:** Iuka is surrounded by outdoor recreation areas. **J.P. Coleman State Park** sits just north of the city on the banks of the Tennessee River and Pickwick Lake, while the popular **Tishomingo State Park** (located along the historic Natchez Trace Parkway) is nearby to the south.\n\n### 2. History & Name\n* **Native American Origins:** The town is named after **Chief Iuka** of the Chickasaw Nation. According to local lore, the chief was cured of a long-standing ailment after drinking from the town\u2019s natural mineral springs.\n* **The Battle of Iuka (Civil War):** On September 19, 1862, the town was the site of the **Battle of Iuka**. It was a fierce, bloody conflict during the American Civil War where Union forces under Major General William S. Rosecrans stopped the advance of Confederate forces led by Major General Sterling Price. \n\n### 3. Mineral Springs Park\nOne of the town's most famous historic landmarks is **Mineral Springs Park**. During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, Iuka was a major resort town because of its natural mineral springs. \n* People traveled by train from all over the country to drink the water, which was believed to have healing properties.\n* The mineral water from these springs actually won the **First Prize for Best Mineral Water** at the **1904 St. Louis World's Fair**. \n* Today, visitors can still stroll through the peaceful park, see the historic springhouses, and visit a preserved historic dogtrot log cabin.\n\n### 4. Demographics & Community\nAs of the 2020 census, Iuka has a quiet, tight-knit population of approximately **3,139 people**. It is known for its small-town Southern charm, historical homes, and slow-paced lifestyle, making it a popular gateway for boaters, anglers, and history buffs visiting Pickwick Lake and the Tennessee River.",
